<section class="band band-soft">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2>What is ME/CFS?</h2>
      <p>Myalgic encephalomyelitis / chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS) is a long-term illness that affects the body's <strong>energy production, immune system and nervous system</strong>. It is not "being tired", and it is not psychological.</p>
      <p>Its defining feature is <strong>post-exertional malaise (PEM)</strong>. When someone does more than their body can handle that day, they crash, often a day or two later, and it can last days, weeks or longer. Exertion includes thinking, talking and emotional stress, not just exercise.</p>
      <p>Routine blood tests usually come back normal, which is why so many people have been dismissed. Research in the last few years has found measurable differences in the brain, blood flow, muscles, immune cells and genes.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="/understanding/">Understand ME/CFS</a> <a class="btn btn-soft" href="/understanding/symptoms/">See the symptoms</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="keymsg">
      <h3>The single most important message</h3>
      <p><strong>Stay inside your energy limits. Rest is treatment, not laziness.</strong></p>
      <p>Pushing past your limits is the most reliable way to get worse. Graded exercise therapy, once standard advice, is now recognised as harmful for ME/CFS. Set your activity for your <em>bad</em> days, not your good ones.</p>
      <p><a href="/living/pacing/">How pacing works →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

